#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128935 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0fad1a9a-7860-5c31-8b3a-e9279f88965b" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Cylindrical Polar Coordinates of a Point"}] :question/id #uuid "45c727f9-ab15-5c6f-a34b-f9b1171e110d" :question/math-academy-id "e-6219" :question/problem "What are the cylindrical polar coordinates of the point $N?$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033855/assets/53fada6c8e77c7c6fa2a6579b52af21ea03839e134fc2cb3d9529f6cfc01bd4b.png)" :question/worked-solution "We write the position of a point in cylindrical coordinates as $(r,\\theta,z),$ where $r \\geq 0$ and $0 \\leq \\theta <         2\\pi.$\n\nAccording to the diagram, we have the following:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033855/assets/d60214a4b32c4462d422372aa90e78944649811ce0b1e277f07fe90663e5be94.png)\n\n  \n\n- The ray from the origin through $N'$ makes an angle of $\\theta = \\dfrac{\\pi}{2} - \\dfrac{\\pi}{3} = \\dfrac{\\pi}{6}$ radians with the positive $x$-axis.\n\n- The distance from the origin to $N'$ is $r=\\sqrt{2}.$\n\n- The $z$-coordinate is $z=3$ because $N$ lies $3$ units *above* the $xy$-plane.\n\nSo, the cylindrical coordinates of $N$ are $\\left(\\sqrt{2}, \\dfrac{\\pi}{6}, 3\\right).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128936 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b16c0bd3-b60d-50dd-b9ea-1ffa93ecb3ab" :knowledge-point/title "Converting From Cartesian Coordinates to Cylindrical Polar Coordinates"}] :question/id #uuid "7ba9f05c-9c5d-518c-bf76-ba1164c49d2d" :question/math-academy-id "e-6220" :question/problem "The point $A$ has Cartesian coordinates $(\\sqrt3,-3,\\sqrt2).$ What are the cylindrical polar coordinates of $A?$" :question/worked-solution "We write the position of a point in cylindrical coordinates as $(r,\\theta,z),$ where $r \\geq 0$ and $0 \\leq \\theta <    2\\pi.$\n\nThe Cartesian coordinates of $A$ are given as $(x,y,z) = (\\sqrt3,-3,\\sqrt2).$\n\n- From these Cartesian coordinates, we know that $z=\\sqrt{2}.$\n\n- To find the values of $r$ and $\\theta,$ we need to find the polar coordinates of the point $(x,y) = (\\sqrt 3, -3)$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nFirst, we find $r,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(\\sqrt{3})}^{2}+(-3)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3+9} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{12} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $(\\sqrt3,-3)$ lies in the fourth  quadrant, we first need to find  the reference angle $\\theta_R\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{R} & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡|\\frac{y}{x}| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡|\\frac{-3}{\\sqrt{3}}| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\sqrt{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we subtract the reference angle from $2\\pi\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta  & =2\\pi -{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =2\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\pi }{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the cylindrical coordinates of $A$ are $\\left(2\\sqrt{3}, \\dfrac{5\\pi}{3}, \\sqrt2 \\right).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128937 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9bb1182f-76ed-514d-808a-94cad16bc2c7" :knowledge-point/title "Converting From Cylindrical Polar Coordinates to Cartesian Coordinates"}] :question/id #uuid "3e024f68-63e5-5080-ba74-c806f6af9036" :question/math-academy-id "e-6221" :question/problem "The point $P$ has cylindrical polar coordinates $\\left(4, \\dfrac{\\pi}{6}, -8 \\right).$ Find the Cartesian coordinates of $P.$" :question/worked-solution "If $(r, \\theta, z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx = r\\cos\\theta, \\qquad\ny = r\\sin\\theta, \\qquad \nz = z.\n$$\n\nCalculating $x$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculating $y$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $P$ are $(2\\sqrt3, 2, -8).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297192 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9bb1182f-76ed-514d-808a-94cad16bc2c7" :knowledge-point/title "Converting From Cylindrical Polar Coordinates to Cartesian Coordinates"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8e69a953-dd80-5431-b0f1-eb4e0406ca3a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2\\sqrt{3},4,-5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297194} {:answer/id #uuid "c0602588-4c8f-544b-870e-fc2ee271fbe7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-4\\sqrt{3},-5,-5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297195} {:answer/id #uuid "e1614bdf-52f2-5bc5-9fa5-675143952b9e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\sqrt{3},2,-5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297196} {:answer/id #uuid "5434c4e9-49c1-57ca-9078-70975dbd6444" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(4\\sqrt{3},4,-5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297197} {:answer/id #uuid "316ad55e-7cc3-5104-b6f0-c46665aee988" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\sqrt{3},-2,-5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297198}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5434c4e9-49c1-57ca-9078-70975dbd6444" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(4\\sqrt{3},4,-5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297197} :answer-field/id #uuid "6a92fb34-2c6a-519b-a97b-65366e953f82" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297193}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "66e7c24c-c992-5cdb-b822-0da7485aaa3f" :question/math-academy-id "q-168896" :question/problem "The point $P$ has cylindrical polar coordinates $(8,\\frac{\\pi }{6},-5).$ Find the Cartesian coordinates of $P.$" :question/worked-solution "If $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z=z\n$$\n\nCalculating $x$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =8\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =8\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculating $y$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =8\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =8\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $P$ are $(4\\sqrt{3},4,-5).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297199 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b16c0bd3-b60d-50dd-b9ea-1ffa93ecb3ab" :knowledge-point/title "Converting From Cartesian Coordinates to Cylindrical Polar Coordinates"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3b0291d7-283a-56e6-b7ac-f79148cbc1fd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\sqrt{2},\\frac{\\pi }{4},2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297201} {:answer/id #uuid "15116dc2-522e-5e62-a131-898c5f0f026c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2\\sqrt{2},\\frac{3\\pi }{4},2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297202} {:answer/id #uuid "73d566aa-eda2-546c-83b0-4b9003fbedad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2\\sqrt{2},\\frac{7\\pi }{4},2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297203} {:answer/id #uuid "37b2667a-8b1e-5fba-9eec-f2350bc4c342" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\sqrt{2},\\frac{3\\pi }{4},2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297204} {:answer/id #uuid "2a4c455f-4e29-54a6-9b22-48753e658e20" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2\\sqrt{2},\\frac{\\pi }{4},2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297205}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "15116dc2-522e-5e62-a131-898c5f0f026c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2\\sqrt{2},\\frac{3\\pi }{4},2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297202} :answer-field/id #uuid "8dbcc958-599e-576f-9487-b626865a1b38" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297200}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0699ef4a-46bc-50f3-8bab-c13f8e76f124" :question/math-academy-id "q-168898" :question/problem "The point $A$ has Cartesian coordinates $(-2,2,2).$ What are the cylindrical polar coordinates of $A?$" :question/worked-solution "We write the position of a point in cylindrical coordinates as $(r,\\theta ,z),$ where $r\\ge 0$ and $0\\le \\theta <2\\pi .$\n\nConsider the point $(x,y)=(-2,2)$ that represents the projection of $(x,y,z)=(-2,2,2)$ onto the $xy$-plane. We need to find the plane polar coordinates $(r,\\theta )$ of $(-2,2).$ Then, the cylindrical coordinates of the point $A$ will be $(r,\\theta ,z).$\n\nFirst, we find $r$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(-2)}^{2}+{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4+4} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{8} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $(-2,2)$ lies in the second quadrant, we first need to find the reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{R} & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡|\\frac{y}{x}| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡|\\frac{2}{-2}| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(1) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we subtract the reference angle from $\\pi :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta  & =\\pi -{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the cylindrical coordinates of $A$ are $(2\\sqrt{2},\\frac{3\\pi }{4},2).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297206 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0fad1a9a-7860-5c31-8b3a-e9279f88965b" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Cylindrical Polar Coordinates of a Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "63e1dd70-a357-58e8-b8ee-e09b32244d33" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,\\frac{7\\pi }{4},-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297208} {:answer/id #uuid "45a3a300-cc2b-5b0c-87dd-c5588a59a6d4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,\\frac{3\\pi }{4},-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297209} {:answer/id #uuid "e53183e0-c5f0-521c-9b7b-6fc7169249b9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,\\frac{7\\pi }{4},1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297210} {:answer/id #uuid "6b344122-6d56-5fb8-bf9f-0f372d4ad6c7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-1,\\frac{\\pi }{4},1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297211} {:answer/id #uuid "11f8d8d6-fa4a-5b61-b8ea-7604c8187285" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,\\frac{\\pi }{4},-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297212}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "63e1dd70-a357-58e8-b8ee-e09b32244d33" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,\\frac{7\\pi }{4},-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297208} :answer-field/id #uuid "0f4b53d1-dcd0-55d1-a450-7fb67bbbda3d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297207}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c3c82b8a-7967-52e2-8482-c631843361f7" :question/math-academy-id "q-74196" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033855/assets/3a919c1ad631af6dfea304db2bdf0ad36fcbff6162f516e8591d7ce9cef3ecca.png)\n\nWhat are the cylindrical polar coordinates of the point $P?$" :question/worked-solution "We write the position of a point in cylindrical coordinates as $(r,\\theta ,z),$ where $r\\ge 0$ and $0\\le \\theta <2\\pi .$\n\nAccording to the diagram, we have the following:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033855/assets/c06f6a453581797596203059e6a1bfd30afd959f469f0408889c6c947a368809.png)\n\n \n\n- The ray from the origin through ${P}^{′}$ makes an angle of $\\theta =2\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{4}=\\frac{7\\pi }{4}$ radians with the positive $x$-axis.\n\n- The distance from the origin to ${P}^{′}$ is $r=1.$\n\n- The $z$-coordinate is $z=1$ because $P$ lies $1$ unit *below* the $xy$-plane.\n\nSo, the cylindrical coordinates of $P$ are $(1,\\frac{7\\pi }{4},-1).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129796 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b16c0bd3-b60d-50dd-b9ea-1ffa93ecb3ab" :knowledge-point/title "Converting From Cartesian Coordinates to Cylindrical Polar Coordinates"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "eb1f6776-1674-5bd7-bc41-5cd3a90f840b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2, \\frac{π}{4}, 5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128571} {:answer/id #uuid "fdb4ad79-6997-5b94-800a-d3c90041f633" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2\\sqrt{2}, \\frac{π}{4}, 5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128572} {:answer/id #uuid "364a29ba-4e0e-5105-8321-cd1033689662" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2\\sqrt{2}, \\frac{3π}{4}, 5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128573} {:answer/id #uuid "5f01eb79-9c4a-5d9a-9ea6-bd4112fb0370" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,-\\frac{7π}{4}, 5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128574} {:answer/id #uuid "63e7b00e-3fcd-5334-9f56-405f03293ce7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2\\sqrt{2}, \\frac{7π}{4}, 5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128575}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "63e7b00e-3fcd-5334-9f56-405f03293ce7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2\\sqrt{2}, \\frac{7π}{4}, 5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128575} :answer-field/id #uuid "2fedb99c-36c2-5604-b5ed-89fa5e1f98d3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128570}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "41fddd1f-73c8-5199-8626-6f5cca7bf9be" :question/math-academy-id "q-74297" :question/problem "The point $A$ has Cartesian coordinates $(2,-2,5).$ What are the cylindrical polar coordinates of $A?$" :question/worked-solution "We write the position of a point in cylindrical coordinates as $(r,\\theta ,z),$ where $r\\ge 0$ and $0\\le \\theta <2\\pi .$\n\nConsider the point $(x,y)=(2,-2)$ that represents the projection of $(x,y,z)=(2,-2,5)$ onto the $xy$-plane. We need to find the plane polar coordinates $(r,\\theta )$ of $(2,-2).$ Then, the cylindrical coordinates of the point $A$ will be $(r,\\theta ,z).$\n\nFirst, we find $r$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{(-2)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4+4} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{8} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $(2,-2)$ lies in the fourth  quadrant, we first need to find  the reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{R} & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡|\\frac{y}{x}| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡|\\frac{-2}{2}| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(1) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we subtract the reference angle from $2\\pi :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta  & =2\\pi -{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =2\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{7\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the cylindrical coordinates of $A$ are $(2\\sqrt{2},\\frac{7\\pi }{4},5).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129798 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9bb1182f-76ed-514d-808a-94cad16bc2c7" :knowledge-point/title "Converting From Cylindrical Polar Coordinates to Cartesian Coordinates"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8ed4ce1c-81f5-5403-ba3f-b119f24c34d7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\sqrt{3}, 2,-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128583} {:answer/id #uuid "9adce2f3-b10c-5eb0-8aed-11cf286dfa2d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-2\\sqrt{3}, 2,-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128584} {:answer/id #uuid "accefd7e-16ff-5652-9c7b-af5a3a11f008" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2\\sqrt{3},-2,-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128585} {:answer/id #uuid "f26331e0-1358-549e-92df-fd09b5422065" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\sqrt{3},-1,-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128586} {:answer/id #uuid "7e32797f-1720-58d5-9662-022ac126f710" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2\\sqrt{3}, 2,-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128587}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7e32797f-1720-58d5-9662-022ac126f710" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2\\sqrt{3}, 2,-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128587} :answer-field/id #uuid "59c6ba03-5ae3-5ace-b3b9-4b33d81b6a22" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128582}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9488b877-c882-5d34-aa2e-ac2ac1e3228c" :question/math-academy-id "q-74299" :question/problem "The point $P$ has cylindrical polar coordinates $(4,\\frac{\\pi }{6},-2).$ Find the Cartesian coordinates of $P.$" :question/worked-solution "If $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z=z\n$$\n\nCalculating $x$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculating $y$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $P$ are $(2\\sqrt{3},2,-2).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129799 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9bb1182f-76ed-514d-808a-94cad16bc2c7" :knowledge-point/title "Converting From Cylindrical Polar Coordinates to Cartesian Coordinates"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c360a395-a1b2-5bc7-9a9c-16bab284bd20" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0, 0, 7)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128589} {:answer/id #uuid "f799400a-2501-5ef8-9818-03548616bd7c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0, 5, 7)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128590} {:answer/id #uuid "fee90ee2-c626-5cc7-8858-23f9b3fc1790" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(5, 0, 7)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128591} {:answer/id #uuid "287720cb-61f0-586d-b13e-837517c6802e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,-5, 7)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128592} {:answer/id #uuid "59ec97cc-b08e-5c15-a17b-72132095a56b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-5, 0, 7)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128593}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "59ec97cc-b08e-5c15-a17b-72132095a56b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-5, 0, 7)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128593} :answer-field/id #uuid "82fb0c0b-442e-5ce6-9e6b-f0bc4c2f52b1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128588}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "66207c82-615e-55c0-a658-14403ee2229e" :question/math-academy-id "q-74300" :question/problem "The point $Q$ has cylindrical coordinates $(5,\\pi ,7).$ Find the Cartesian coordinates of $Q.$" :question/worked-solution "If $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z=z\n$$\n\nCalculating $x$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi ) \\\\  & =5\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculating $y$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi ) \\\\  & =5\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $Q$ are $(-5,0,7).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129805 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0fad1a9a-7860-5c31-8b3a-e9279f88965b" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Cylindrical Polar Coordinates of a Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "242050d2-27c0-5af9-8775-a66205ea4215" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1, \\frac{4π}{5},-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128625} {:answer/id #uuid "2fcb8acc-31a0-5f5e-a8df-605eee9eafbc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1, \\frac{π}{5},-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128626} {:answer/id #uuid "52a25b01-68dc-5617-b55b-37f67e0a9362" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,-\\frac{π}{5}, 1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128627} {:answer/id #uuid "6eceba01-b47e-5ffc-96b8-3cb2d7e15762" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,-\\frac{π}{5},-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128628} {:answer/id #uuid "72225c09-321e-5ac0-b836-090e0ce58da4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1, \\frac{π}{5}, 1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128629}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2fcb8acc-31a0-5f5e-a8df-605eee9eafbc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1, \\frac{π}{5},-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128626} :answer-field/id #uuid "0d0dd1c0-b3e9-599f-bd12-23425246d081" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128624}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6dd48db5-e566-59eb-ae0c-ebf7461fdbb5" :question/math-academy-id "q-74430" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033855/assets/dcf326a7962c01814c937e07083193a107597e1995fb66dffd5776a40cf07a9f.png)\n\nWhat are the cylindrical polar coordinates of the point $F?$" :question/worked-solution "We write the position of a point in cylindrical coordinates as $(r,\\theta ,z),$ where $r\\ge 0$ and $0\\le \\theta <2\\pi .$\n\nAccording to the diagram, we have the following:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033855/assets/189c1f77e7d2dfd97f51e05f4be55423d100921d2dcad5cafe4be4d90463e8d8.png)\n\n \n\n- The ray from the origin through ${F}^{′}$ makes an angle of $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{5}$ radians with the positive $x$-axis.\n\n- The distance from the origin to ${F}^{′}$ is $r=1.$\n\n- The $z$-coordinate is $z=-1$ because $F$ lies $1$ unit *below* the $xy$-plane.\n\nSo, the cylindrical coordinates of $F$ are $(1,\\frac{\\pi }{5},-1).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297213 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0fad1a9a-7860-5c31-8b3a-e9279f88965b" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Cylindrical Polar Coordinates of a Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "72fcdd34-76db-546b-8f64-0d10d3265758" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\sqrt{3},\\frac{\\pi }{6},2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297215} {:answer/id #uuid "068ecdfa-0222-58f3-99f8-a976c7ba15b9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3,\\frac{\\pi }{3},2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297216} {:answer/id #uuid "a8961439-ec9c-5d8e-a94f-a728a2225ea6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\sqrt{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297217} {:answer/id #uuid "06cb26d4-a720-526c-b64d-913efcc9462a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,\\frac{\\pi }{6},-\\sqrt{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297218} {:answer/id #uuid "53e57438-ec6b-50d3-b053-d241f6947281" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,\\frac{\\pi }{3},-\\sqrt{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297219}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a8961439-ec9c-5d8e-a94f-a728a2225ea6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\sqrt{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297217} :answer-field/id #uuid "fbd753ca-fbfc-5a86-87cf-c0d7810a4541" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297214}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4d042fb9-aa57-556a-8a5e-0719c71e7350" :question/math-academy-id "q-74455" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033855/assets/b8f187c949db780dc4ef62360c76be4a952e2c299257c6d4e9ee80c7254605d6.png)\n\nWhat are the cylindrical polar coordinates of the point $W?$" :question/worked-solution "We write the position of a point in cylindrical coordinates as $(r,\\theta ,z),$ where $r\\ge 0$ and $0\\le \\theta <2\\pi .$\n\nAccording to the diagram, we have the following:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033855/assets/d1855e0e8f1b8feed4eb2f5fb9dd2a1abadd0ee92304ae0460f9bd130e376b04.png)\n\n  \n\n- The ray from the origin through ${W}^{′}$ makes an angle of $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{2}-\\frac{\\pi }{6}=\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ radians with the positive $x$-axis.\n\n- The distance from the origin to ${W}^{′}$ is $r=2.$\n\n- The $z$-coordinate is $z=\\sqrt{3}$ because $W$ lies $\\sqrt{3}$ units *above* the $xy$-plane.\n\nSo, the cylindrical coordinates of $W$ are $(2,\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\sqrt{3}).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297220 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0fad1a9a-7860-5c31-8b3a-e9279f88965b" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Cylindrical Polar Coordinates of a Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "70a9eeb9-26d9-5b4e-a329-5d37c3c158b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{4}{5},\\frac{5\\pi }{4},-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297222} {:answer/id #uuid "99927f45-57a4-5e55-aaef-472f1ccee159" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{4}{5},\\frac{\\pi }{4},2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297223} {:answer/id #uuid "a7d217c7-7a90-508d-8f8a-9260d4e9cbc5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{5}{4},\\frac{5\\pi }{4},2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297224} {:answer/id #uuid "4cb17452-332e-5cf4-bdea-ff585699fa77" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{5}{4},\\frac{\\pi }{4},2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297225} {:answer/id #uuid "781fde25-1a84-56ab-8b9d-a1efb7e381c9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{3}{4},\\frac{4\\pi }{5},2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297226}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a7d217c7-7a90-508d-8f8a-9260d4e9cbc5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{5}{4},\\frac{5\\pi }{4},2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297224} :answer-field/id #uuid "1334cdea-bafa-51db-b8b2-6795b8974fc3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297221}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4690f5df-0d5c-5893-b967-ee1c189586a6" :question/math-academy-id "q-74456" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033855/assets/c6de44233dec37e695e70902e0dace0771dd42a4cb87b45205345ba431e7774c.png)\n\nWhat are the cylindrical polar coordinates of the point $K?$" :question/worked-solution "We write the position of a point in cylindrical coordinates as $(r,\\theta ,z),$ where $r\\ge 0$ and $0\\le \\theta <2\\pi .$\n\nAccording to the diagram, we have the following:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033855/assets/7c49639200c77c264ecb7b58e1eda4053b3ab0fe378d35caa3d3f8bdc6ad5027.png)\n\n  \n\n- The ray from the origin through ${K}^{′}$ makes an angle of $\\theta =\\frac{3\\pi }{2}-\\frac{\\pi }{4}=\\frac{5\\pi }{4}$ radians with the positive $x$-axis.\n\n- The distance from the origin to ${K}^{′}$ is $r=\\frac{5}{4}.$\n\n- The $z$-coordinate is $z=2$ because $K$ lies $2$ units *above* the $xy$-plane.\n\nSo, the cylindrical coordinates of $K$ are $(\\frac{5}{4},\\frac{5\\pi }{4},2).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297227 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0fad1a9a-7860-5c31-8b3a-e9279f88965b" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Cylindrical Polar Coordinates of a Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d84253b9-98e5-5eea-b9e1-7fb55fa72025" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,\\frac{5\\pi }{3},\\sqrt{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297229} {:answer/id #uuid "116ee320-b30c-51b0-8cf5-78850e685f34" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\sqrt{2},\\frac{5\\pi }{3},-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297230} {:answer/id #uuid "bbed4d0a-26d9-515a-9d79-a7d7832f4094" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,\\frac{5\\pi }{3},2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297231} {:answer/id #uuid "3a9e9c0c-df90-5ed5-a7f6-832af8b695da" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\sqrt{2},\\frac{\\pi }{3},2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297232} {:answer/id #uuid "7bb8e3d2-ba93-5ec3-b22d-8bbbccf865ef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-2,\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\sqrt{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297233}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "116ee320-b30c-51b0-8cf5-78850e685f34" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\sqrt{2},\\frac{5\\pi }{3},-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297230} :answer-field/id #uuid "e66826c8-afe5-5efe-94e7-526b9beb74e1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297228}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "20767a75-5369-5236-acee-13aa15c1cb10" :question/math-academy-id "q-74460" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033855/assets/947857af9d5746ceef623a5d2cc2bf0ac82e6570d7f288727a7591278fc50452.png)\n\nWhat are the cylindrical polar coordinates of the point $M?$" :question/worked-solution "We write the position of a point in cylindrical coordinates as $(r,\\theta ,z),$ where $r\\ge 0$ and $0\\le \\theta <2\\pi .$\n\nAccording to the diagram, we have the following:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033855/assets/88aeecb46c1f996a7a2a8947a8b5262faa2991992771a960a1e806c2b8915cac.png)\n\n \n\n- The ray from the origin through ${M}^{′}$ makes an angle of $\\theta =2\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{3}=\\frac{5\\pi }{3}$ radians with the positive $x$-axis.\n\n- The distance from the origin to ${M}^{′}$ is $r=\\sqrt{2}.$\n\n- The $z$-coordinate is $z=2$ because $M$ lies $2$ units *below* the $xy$-plane.\n\nSo, the cylindrical coordinates of $M$ are $(\\sqrt{2},\\frac{5\\pi }{3},-2).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297234 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9bb1182f-76ed-514d-808a-94cad16bc2c7" :knowledge-point/title "Converting From Cylindrical Polar Coordinates to Cartesian Coordinates"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b345d5d0-7b33-5a68-aef4-9a82ef18e7b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,1,-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297236} {:answer/id #uuid "ebda8b4b-32d9-5a53-b46d-2d31fdf7f3f8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-1,\\sqrt{2},-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297237} {:answer/id #uuid "61333079-e421-5a0f-a542-47d9829ae153" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},1,-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297238} {:answer/id #uuid "2de1832a-32f9-5914-8ee4-e626bacc96d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297239} {:answer/id #uuid "ac821426-993d-5140-b310-492168dce918" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\sqrt{2},1,-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297240}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b345d5d0-7b33-5a68-aef4-9a82ef18e7b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,1,-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297236} :answer-field/id #uuid "26bcc3a3-820b-5cf4-85a0-b5ee8364921f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297235}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f9c23e7d-94ca-5a68-9212-337e99ab62b9" :question/math-academy-id "q-74469" :question/problem "The point $P$ has cylindrical polar coordinates $(\\sqrt{2},\\frac{\\pi }{4},-1).$ Find the Cartesian coordinates of $P.$" :question/worked-solution "If $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z=z\n$$\n\nCalculating $x$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculating $y$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $P$ are $(1,1,-1).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297241 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9bb1182f-76ed-514d-808a-94cad16bc2c7" :knowledge-point/title "Converting From Cylindrical Polar Coordinates to Cartesian Coordinates"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "266c9573-a4de-5acd-a642-a29005a8422d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,\\sqrt{2},-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297243} {:answer/id #uuid "7ce8601e-ac9f-5552-ba08-f5ca7454d79c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-2\\sqrt{2},-2,-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297244} {:answer/id #uuid "631a3e13-3f7b-5f39-a7aa-773a196e092a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,2,-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297245} {:answer/id #uuid "d4f8a570-c9e8-53f2-acf0-3cdd52caaaaf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-2,2,-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297246} {:answer/id #uuid "25569dc0-0eca-59e1-bd9e-eacd25bcf060" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,-2\\sqrt{2},-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297247}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d4f8a570-c9e8-53f2-acf0-3cdd52caaaaf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-2,2,-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297246} :answer-field/id #uuid "63ea183d-3390-5597-9df7-e59224d62a1a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297242}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2bfca49b-1cfe-599f-b76c-99ada9ea4dad" :question/math-academy-id "q-74474" :question/problem "The point $Q$ has cylindrical coordinates $(2\\sqrt{2},\\frac{3\\pi }{4},-3).$ Find the Cartesian coordinates of $Q.$" :question/worked-solution "If $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z=z\n$$\n\nCalculating $x$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}\\cdot (\\frac{-\\sqrt{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculating $y$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}\\cdot (\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $Q$ are $(-2,2,-3).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297248 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b16c0bd3-b60d-50dd-b9ea-1ffa93ecb3ab" :knowledge-point/title "Converting From Cartesian Coordinates to Cylindrical Polar Coordinates"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8be40b28-bc94-5725-b520-bd13f6a6497e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,\\frac{7\\pi }{6},2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297250} {:answer/id #uuid "b8fce348-ddfa-55f7-9dad-496a9fa435c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,\\frac{7\\pi }{6},2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297251} {:answer/id #uuid "95befea1-e010-520e-984e-1f142b758c3e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,\\frac{4\\pi }{3},2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297252} {:answer/id #uuid "99e1c33a-5677-5bbe-9e9a-13d32494e7d2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,\\frac{5\\pi }{4},2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297253} {:answer/id #uuid "d7281004-716f-59b6-9c04-f82fe04088f2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,\\frac{5\\pi }{4},2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297254}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "95befea1-e010-520e-984e-1f142b758c3e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,\\frac{4\\pi }{3},2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297252} :answer-field/id #uuid "3bac72af-705c-53cc-a679-e95f5e2f1ca1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297249}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "df5c8142-fcaf-5796-8871-1ba977c3d88a" :question/math-academy-id "q-74478" :question/problem "The point $A$ has Cartesian coordinates $(-1,-\\sqrt{3},2).$ What are the cylindrical polar coordinates of $A?$" :question/worked-solution "We write the position of a point in cylindrical coordinates as $(r,\\theta ,z),$ where $r\\ge 0$ and $0\\le \\theta <2\\pi .$\n\nConsider the point $(x,y)=(-1,-\\sqrt{3})$ that represents the projection of $(x,y,z)=(-1,-\\sqrt{3},2)$ onto the $xy$-plane. We need to find the plane polar coordinates $(r,\\theta )$ of $(-1,-\\sqrt{3}).$ Then, the cylindrical coordinates of the point $A$ will be $(r,\\theta ,z).$ \n\nFirst, we find $r$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+{(-\\sqrt{3})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+3} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, since $(-1,-\\sqrt{3})$ lies in the third  quadrant, we first need to find  the reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{R} & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡|\\frac{y}{x}| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡|\\frac{-\\sqrt{3}}{-1}| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\sqrt{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we add the reference angle and $\\pi :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta  & =\\pi +{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =\\pi +\\frac{\\pi }{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\pi }{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the cylindrical coordinates of $A$ are $(2,\\frac{4\\pi }{3},2).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297255 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b16c0bd3-b60d-50dd-b9ea-1ffa93ecb3ab" :knowledge-point/title "Converting From Cartesian Coordinates to Cylindrical Polar Coordinates"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2065a636-3bd8-5201-977a-317244519985" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\sqrt{5},3.141,-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297257} {:answer/id #uuid "3930bb12-d564-51ce-ba01-3b4f0206a904" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\sqrt{5},1.571,-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297258} {:answer/id #uuid "7c251fc3-ee38-5557-8023-971cd62d5ac5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(5,2.214,-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297259} {:answer/id #uuid "394e1e0d-82eb-5691-95de-25572ca9515e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\sqrt{10},3.141,-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297260} {:answer/id #uuid "321617ec-a2ed-5746-9da6-e66b5966a4f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(5,0.785,-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297261}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7c251fc3-ee38-5557-8023-971cd62d5ac5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(5,2.214,-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297259} :answer-field/id #uuid "3301c95f-3317-5628-8466-1e3972182a86" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297256}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2cc9f6fd-6cbc-5289-99ad-a9d6b26e5b99" :question/math-academy-id "q-74479" :question/problem "The point $C$ has Cartesian coordinates $(-3,4,-1).$ What are the cylindrical polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ,z)$ of $C?$ Express $\\theta $ in radians rounded to three decimal places." :question/worked-solution "We write the position of a point in cylindrical coordinates as $(r,\\theta ,z),$ where $r\\ge 0$ and $0\\le \\theta <2\\pi .$ \n\nConsider the point $(x,y)=(-3,4)$ that represents the projection of $(x,y,z)=(-3,4,-1)$ onto the $xy$-plane. We need to find the plane polar coordinates $(r,\\theta )$ of $(-3,4).$ Then, the cylindrical coordinates of the point $C$ will be $(r,\\theta ,z).$\n\nFirst, we find $r$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-3)^{2}+{4}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9+16} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{25} \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $(-3,4)$ lies in the second quadrant, we first need to find  the reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{R} & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡|\\frac{y}{x}| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡|\\frac{4}{-3}| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{4}{3}) \\\\  & ≈0.927\\,295\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we subtract the reference angle from $\\pi :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta  & =\\pi -{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & ≈\\pi -0.927\\,295 \\\\  & =2.214\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the cylindrical coordinates of $C$ are $(5,2.214,-1).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297262 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b16c0bd3-b60d-50dd-b9ea-1ffa93ecb3ab" :knowledge-point/title "Converting From Cartesian Coordinates to Cylindrical Polar Coordinates"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f62909b4-7946-5032-917f-c6ded9d81a96" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2\\sqrt{3},\\frac{\\pi }{6},-3)." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297264} {:answer/id #uuid "eba853e7-2c91-5f73-bc90-a114a4866028" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2\\sqrt{3},\\frac{11\\pi }{6},-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297265} {:answer/id #uuid "374cad50-3ed0-57a6-bb68-8586e90e0d93" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\sqrt{3},\\frac{7\\pi }{6},-3)." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297266} {:answer/id #uuid "60b0435b-a8bf-5fcd-a656-01b976ac568a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3\\sqrt{3},\\frac{5\\pi }{6},-3)." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297267} {:answer/id #uuid "2822ceec-5e60-5acd-ae73-6eb49761f406" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\sqrt{3},\\frac{5\\pi }{6},-3)." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297268}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "eba853e7-2c91-5f73-bc90-a114a4866028" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2\\sqrt{3},\\frac{11\\pi }{6},-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297265} :answer-field/id #uuid "d20f7b61-16da-5d4a-aec6-c9ade57c8a1b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297263}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1f1545fd-077a-5d15-8508-132b073ff140" :question/math-academy-id "q-74482" :question/problem "The point $P$ has Cartesian coordinates $(3,-\\sqrt{3},-3).$ What are the cylindrical polar coordinates of $P?$" :question/worked-solution "We write the position of a point in cylindrical coordinates as $(r,\\theta ,z),$ where $r\\ge 0$ and $0\\le \\theta <2\\pi .$ \n\nConsider the point $(x,y)=(3,-\\sqrt{3})$ that represents the projection of $(x,y,z)=(3,-\\sqrt{3},-3)$ onto the $xy$-plane. We need to find the plane polar coordinates $(r,\\theta )$ of $(3,-\\sqrt{3}).$ Then, the cylindrical coordinates of the point $P$ will be $(r,\\theta ,z).$\n\nFirst, we find $r$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+{(-\\sqrt{3})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9+3} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{12} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $(3,-\\sqrt{3})$ lies in the fourth  quadrant, we first need to find  the reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{R} & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡|\\frac{y}{x}| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡|\\frac{-\\sqrt{3}}{3}| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we subtract the reference angle from $2\\pi :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta  & =2\\pi -{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =2\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{11\\pi }{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the cylindrical coordinates of $P$ are $(2\\sqrt{3},\\frac{11\\pi }{6},-3).$"}]}
